### 💻 Code

```ts
type Option<T> = Some<T> | None;
interface Some<T> {
    _tag: "some",
    value: T
}

interface None {
    _tag: "none"
}

type ComparableBase = number | boolean | null | undefined | symbol | bigint;

type Comparable<T> = T extends ComparableBase ? true : ( T extends Option<infer U> ? Comparable<U> : false);

type A = Comparable<number>
type B = Comparable<Option<number>>
type C = Comparable<Option<Option<number>>>
type D = Comparable<string>
type E = Comparable<Option<string>>
type F = Comparable<Option<Option<string>>>

// type ReverseDeduction<T> = Comparable<T> extends false ? never : any; // works
type ReverseDeduction<T> = Comparable<T> extends true ? any : never; // not works

class TMustComparable<T extends ReverseDeduction<T>> {
    value: T;
    constructor(x: T) {
        this.value = x;
    }
}

// let a = new TMustComparable<string>("aaa"); // => should error
let b = new TMustComparable<number>(123);
let c = new TMustComparable<Option<number>>({_tag: "some", value:123}); // should not error
let d = new TMustComparable<Option<Option<number>>>({ _tag: "some", value: {_tag: "some", value: 123}}); // should not error
// let e = new TMustComparable<Option<string>>({_tag: "some", value:"aaa"}); // => should error

```


### 🙁 Actual behavior

The following errors are given
```
Type 'Option<number>' does not satisfy the constraint 'never'.
  Type 'None' is not assignable to type 'never'.
Type 'Option<Option<number>>' does not satisfy the constraint 'never'.
  Type 'None' is not assignable to type 'never'.
```
it's because the type of `Comparable<Option<number>>` is calcuated as `boolean`  (result of `true | false` I think, looks like something here about `?:` is being treated as OR ) instead of `true`, similar happens to `Comparable<Option<Option<number>>>`

### 🙂 Expected behavior

The code should compile. Actually if you replace line 24 with line 23, it works. but in the end I think the type system should be able to accurately deduct the type to either true or false, instead of true, false and boolean in this case.
Although both cases will go through `T extends Option<infer U> ? Comparable<U>`, the false case -- `Comparable<Option<string>>` will have no problem being recognized as `false` in both cases.  

